WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.610
Start with three.

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:03.490
Add two and then double.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:05.170
Five becomes ten.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.050
Try the same start with the operations swapped.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:10.790
Double three first, then add two.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:12.350
Six becomes eight.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.940
The same steps can give different results when

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:15.850
their order changes.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.140
Testing more starting numbers might help us

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:19.910
guess a pattern.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.140
A letter lets us explain the pattern for

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:23.410
every starting number.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.800
We'll compare the two operation orders side by side and

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:28.050
look for the part that changes.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:30.730
Choose any starting number.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.210
Order A adds five and then doubles.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.930
Order B doubles and then adds five.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:39.010
Which order gives the bigger result, and by how much?

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.090
Try one starting number before we compare them all.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:47.720
Call the starting number x.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.870
In Order A, adding five makes a bar with an x part and

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:52.820
a five part.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:55.540
Doubling makes two copies of that whole bar.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:56.740
Collect its parts.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.720
Two copies of x and two fives give two x plus ten.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.080
Order B doubles x first.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.160
That gives two copies of x.

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:06.700
Add five once.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.350
Both orders now show two copies of the starting value

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:11.800
and at least one five.

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:13.540
Pair those matching pieces.

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.860
The extra five in Order A is the entire difference.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.270
What if we multiply by negative two instead?

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.440
In Order A, the five is multiplied too,

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.510
so its contribution is negative ten.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:28.370
Order B still adds positive five.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.990
The common part is negative two x in both orders.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.470
Positive five is fifteen more than negative ten,

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.990
so Order B now wins by fifteen.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.900
Two additions can swap because we still add

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:42.750
the same total.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:46.000
Two multiplications can swap because their combined

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.330
scale is the same.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.980
When you mix the two types of step, look closely at

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.610
whether an amount added later gets scaled at all.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:55.490
Give the start a letter.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:57.430
Write the two orders separately.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:01:59.700
Compare the parts that match and

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:00.830
calculate the difference.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.500
In your notebook, compare subtracting four and then

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.940
multiplying by three with multiplying by three and

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:09.190
then subtracting four.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.950
Which gives the greater result, and by how much?
